    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The strongest argument against the Electoral College is that it allows the winner of a national popular vote to lose the presidency, which undermines the fundamental principle of \"one person, one vote.\" Critics also note that the system effectively creates 50 or more mini-campaigns focused solely on \"battleground states,\" discouraging candidate engagement in large, safe vote banks. Furthermore, the college is viewed by many as a legacy of the 18th and 19th centuries that no longer reflects the democratic ideals of the modern, 21st-century United States. By advocating for the Popular Vote, you can argue for a government that truly represents the will of the entire electorate without the complexities of state-level distortions.",
